        {
          "id": "sha256:2b4db81d6923206a1363f86e17cf3905fd13324d62b4a0d732afda79f770604d",
          "source_id": "ORIGINAL-0001",
          "path": "original-0001.html",
          "hash": "sha256:93d03d3493443fc88a7926765b45deac1cfc15592e9bf03cc27b5a2c737fe124",
          "body_start": 2043,
          "text": "By May 1944, over 2,876,000 Allied troops were amassed in southern England. While awaiting deployment orders, they prepared for the assault by practicing with live ammunition. The largest armada in history, made up of more than 4,000 American, British, and Canadian ships, lay in wait. More that 1,200 planes stood ready to deliver seasoned airborne troops behind enemy lines, to silence German ground resistance as best they could, and to dominate the skies of the impending battle theater.",
          "body_projection": "body-text-no-headings-v1",
          "context_truncated": false,
